  "@lesson1Card1Speech": { "description": "Lesson text. Words in `backticks` are code: keep them exactly as they are, in English." },
  "lesson1Card1Title": "What is a variable?",
  "lesson1Card1Speech": "Beep boop! Every robot needs to remember things. Me too! I keep my battery level in a variable. A variable is like a box with a label on it. The label is its name. The thing inside the box is its value.",
  "lesson1Card1Tip": "A variable is a labeled box that stores a value.",

  "lesson1Card2Title": "Making a variable",
  "lesson1Card2Speech": "Let's make one! First write the name. Then write `=`. Then write the value. The `=` sign means \"put this in the box.\" So this line puts 100 in a box called `battery`.",
  "lesson1Card2Tip": "In Python, `=` does not mean \"equals\" like in math. It means \"store this value.\"",

  "lesson1Card3Title": "Different kinds of values",
  "lesson1Card3Speech": "A box can hold different kinds of values. It can hold a number, like `5`. It can hold words inside quotes, like `\"Cog\"`. It can also hold `True` or `False`, like a yes or no answer.",
  "lesson1Card3Tip": "Words always need quotes. Numbers, `True` and `False` don't. `True` and `False` always start with a capital letter.",

  "lesson1Card4Title": "Changing a variable",
  "lesson1Card4Speech": "Oh no, I walked a lot and used some power! Let's change my battery. Python reads code from top to bottom. First the box gets 100. Then it gets 80. The old value is gone. Now the box holds 80.",
  "lesson1Card4Tip": "A variable only keeps its newest value.",

  "lesson1Card5Title": "Naming rules",
  "lesson1Card5Speech": "Every box needs a good name. Here are my rules:\n• No spaces. Use an underscore `_` instead.\n• Don't start with a number.\n• Uppercase and lowercase are different. `speed` and `Speed` are two different boxes!\n• Pick a name that makes sense.",
  "lesson1Card5Tip": "Good names make your code easy to read, for you and your friends!",

  "@quiz1Q1Prompt": { "description": "Quiz text. Hints (…Why…) must be gentle, 1-2 short sentences, and must not reveal the right answer. Words in `backticks` are code: keep them in English." },
  "quiz1Q1Prompt": "What is a variable?",
  "quiz1Q1Right": "A labeled box that stores a value",
  "quiz1Q1WrongRobot": "A type of robot",
  "quiz1Q1WhyRobot": "Not quite! A variable isn't a robot. It's something a robot uses to remember things.",
  "quiz1Q1WrongCommand": "A command that turns the robot on",
  "quiz1Q1WhyCommand": "Good guess, but a variable doesn't do an action. It holds something.",
  "quiz1Q1Explain": "A variable is like a labeled box. The label is the name, and the value goes inside.",

  "quiz1Q2Prompt": "In this code, what does the `=` sign do?",
  "quiz1Q2Right": "It puts the value `5` into the variable `speed`",
  "quiz1Q2WrongCheck": "It checks if `speed` is equal to `5`",
  "quiz1Q2WhyCheck": "That's what `=` means in math! But Python uses it in a different way.",
  "quiz1Q2WrongDelete": "It deletes `speed`",
  "quiz1Q2WhyDelete": "Nothing gets deleted here. Think about the box!",
  "quiz1Q2Explain": "In Python, `=` means \"store this value.\" So `5` goes into the box called `speed`.",

  "quiz1Q3Prompt": "What kind of value is this?",
